1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,134
I'm Sujan Challa, a senior software
engineer in CTV advertising space.

2
00:00:05,728 --> 00:00:11,029
I design, build, and operate the
real-time decision system that

3
00:00:11,047 --> 00:00:13,032
choose, like which ad you see.

4
00:00:13,985 --> 00:00:18,834
This talk is about, what platform
engineering looks like, when the latency

5
00:00:19,091 --> 00:00:22,735
budget is not a guideline but a hard wall.

6
00:00:23,496 --> 00:00:28,103
A real-time bidding auction that clears
like end-to-end in about a hundred

7
00:00:28,103 --> 00:00:30,949
milliseconds, millions of times a day.

8
00:00:31,536 --> 00:00:35,482
I'll walk through the constraints,
in the order they forced themselves

9
00:00:35,483 --> 00:00:39,794
on us because, that's the honest
way this architecture was built.

10
00:00:40,344 --> 00:00:42,767
One deadline, one force move at a time.

11
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:45,594
Quick context, before the case study.

12
00:00:45,656 --> 00:00:49,408
Like, what does the team behind
a system like this actually own?

13
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:51,750
The slide, says it in one line.

14
00:00:52,008 --> 00:00:56,542
My job turns business goals into
a reliable, decision system.

15
00:00:57,179 --> 00:01:00,977
design the business says,
maximize revenue, respond in

16
00:01:01,003 --> 00:01:05,416
100 milliseconds, respect user
privacy, don't break the product.

17
00:01:05,506 --> 00:01:10,933
We translate that into service boundaries,
data contracts, and, latency budgets.

18
00:01:11,634 --> 00:01:17,448
Build the part you would expect,
like Go services, CI/CD pipeline, ML

19
00:01:17,448 --> 00:01:22,219
serving paths, APIs, and integrations
with, TV apps and our partners.

20
00:01:23,519 --> 00:01:24,166
Operate.

21
00:01:24,644 --> 00:01:30,311
After launch, we own the latency
graphs, release safety, observability,

22
00:01:30,319 --> 00:01:33,101
incidents, and technical debt ledger.

23
00:01:33,565 --> 00:01:36,673
That's where the platform
engineering earns its name.

24
00:01:37,525 --> 00:01:39,362
And the last is collaborate.

25
00:01:39,443 --> 00:01:41,039
None of this is a solo work.

26
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:46,070
It, crosses product managers, data
scientists and operations, client

27
00:01:46,074 --> 00:01:48,321
teams, SREs, and partner engineers.

28
00:01:48,717 --> 00:01:52,456
The architecture is half the job,
and the half is in alignment,

29
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:53,894
within the other teams.

30
00:01:54,894 --> 00:01:56,767
And none of it is a handoff.

31
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:59,685
Production software moves
in a loop, like four steps.

32
00:02:00,279 --> 00:02:02,201
step one, frame the outcome.

33
00:02:02,406 --> 00:02:07,168
Product and engineering agree on the user
value, the constraint, and the metric,

34
00:02:07,194 --> 00:02:12,958
something like increase auction win rate,
by 2% at, P99 under 100 milliseconds.

35
00:02:13,128 --> 00:02:14,006
That's a framing.

36
00:02:14,814 --> 00:02:17,658
Step two, make decision visible.

37
00:02:18,229 --> 00:02:22,500
Architecture reviews and design
docs force the trade-offs into the

38
00:02:22,577 --> 00:02:25,067
open before anyone writes code.

39
00:02:26,793 --> 00:02:29,533
Step three, deliver in small increments.

40
00:02:29,963 --> 00:02:34,532
Backlogs, sprints, code reviews,
automated tests, every increment

41
00:02:34,544 --> 00:02:36,527
small enough, so we can roll back.

42
00:02:37,511 --> 00:02:40,026
Step four, release with, guardrails.

43
00:02:40,549 --> 00:02:45,041
Canary environments, metric
gates, rollback paths, dashboards,

44
00:02:45,088 --> 00:02:48,688
and then on-call feedback
flows right back into step one.

45
00:02:49,156 --> 00:02:50,789
Keep that loop in mind.

46
00:02:50,789 --> 00:02:56,736
Like auction system I'm about to show you
went through it dozens of times, and every

47
00:02:56,736 --> 00:02:59,374
constraint in the talk arrived through it.

48
00:03:00,374 --> 00:03:02,868
Now the case study one ad slot opens.

49
00:03:02,973 --> 00:03:06,815
A website or an app offers a
single impression for sale.

50
00:03:07,903 --> 00:03:12,505
50 plus systems each decide
what this user, this context

51
00:03:12,505 --> 00:03:15,152
is worth, pick a price and bid.

52
00:03:15,794 --> 00:03:19,968
The auction clears, the highest
eligible bid wins the slot.

53
00:03:20,695 --> 00:03:26,027
And the number that governs everything is
like 100 milliseconds request to response.

54
00:03:26,074 --> 00:03:30,462
An entire market clears before
the page feels like loading

55
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:34,326
The 100 milliseconds is
complete round, round trip.

56
00:03:34,896 --> 00:03:38,774
The network eats part of it
before any useful work begins.

57
00:03:39,317 --> 00:03:43,652
The request and the bid cross the
network before, useful work starts.

58
00:03:44,211 --> 00:03:49,570
And the consequences, the governing
rule of this whole talk, a lot-- a

59
00:03:49,580 --> 00:03:51,549
late answer is not a slower answer.

60
00:03:51,549 --> 00:03:52,969
It's a no answer.

61
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:55,874
The exchange closes the
auction without you.

62
00:03:56,609 --> 00:04:01,926
So the first design everyone sketches,
add request, single server, database

63
00:04:01,926 --> 00:04:04,576
lookup, bid is impossible here.

64
00:04:04,868 --> 00:04:10,527
A database call per request multiplies
load, and one slow dependency sets

65
00:04:10,529 --> 00:04:12,515
the whole system latency flow.

66
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:16,314
The deadline forces the
first architectural move

67
00:04:17,313 --> 00:04:22,280
Move one, put a feature, feature
cache in front of everything.

68
00:04:22,367 --> 00:04:26,945
The bid request reads from the cache,
and the decision happens from the memory.

69
00:04:27,422 --> 00:04:28,734
What becomes fast?

70
00:04:28,817 --> 00:04:32,845
User context, campaign, and
model inputs come from memory

71
00:04:32,858 --> 00:04:34,471
instead of a database round trip.

72
00:04:35,110 --> 00:04:36,807
What becomes a trade-off?

73
00:04:36,871 --> 00:04:40,994
The data may be slightly stale,
so expiration, invalidation,

74
00:04:41,001 --> 00:04:45,258
and source of truth recovery are
designed, not improvi-improvised.

75
00:04:46,097 --> 00:04:51,283
The architecture choice at the
bottom reserve strict consistency

76
00:04:51,288 --> 00:04:54,872
for the facts that truly require
it, like, budget accounting.

77
00:04:55,312 --> 00:04:58,209
Speed beats perfect
freshness, on the live path

78
00:04:59,209 --> 00:05:02,329
Move two, parallelize
the independent waits.

79
00:05:02,716 --> 00:05:06,414
The bidder fans out to user,
budget, and model all at once.

80
00:05:06,841 --> 00:05:11,493
The slowest bounded lookup sets the
join time, not the sum of every lookup.

81
00:05:12,404 --> 00:05:16,716
The third move is to
scale replicas, not state.

82
00:05:17,118 --> 00:05:20,272
A router sends the request to
any healthy bidder instance.

83
00:05:20,837 --> 00:05:23,591
Regions add capacity near demand.

84
00:05:23,779 --> 00:05:28,134
Any healthy instance can serve any
request, so the capacity is added

85
00:05:28,135 --> 00:05:30,810
as replicas, not, desi-- redesigns.

86
00:05:31,490 --> 00:05:34,079
Generalize this for your own platform.

87
00:05:34,099 --> 00:05:38,969
Under a strict budget, every synchronous
hop is a purchase made with milliseconds.

88
00:05:39,788 --> 00:05:44,246
Precomputation and locality are
how you stop paying your retail

89
00:05:45,246 --> 00:05:48,396
CTV ads a wrinkle web never had.

90
00:05:48,888 --> 00:05:50,239
Ads arrive in pods.

91
00:05:50,336 --> 00:05:55,320
A commercial break is several impressions
that must be decided together.

92
00:05:56,040 --> 00:06:02,871
Two car ads back-to-back or the same ad
twice in one break is a failure the im-

93
00:06:02,994 --> 00:06:05,467
per impression auction can't even see.

94
00:06:06,279 --> 00:06:10,266
The auction core, though, only
understands single impressions,

95
00:06:10,393 --> 00:06:15,033
so the platform decomposes each
pod into plain impressions.

96
00:06:15,489 --> 00:06:19,970
The core understands, runs
the auction, the reassemble…

97
00:06:20,690 --> 00:06:25,391
then reassembles the pod itself,
like assignment, deal tri- tiers,

98
00:06:25,757 --> 00:06:29,073
competitive separation, and
final assembly of the response.

99
00:06:29,850 --> 00:06:33,990
The platform lesson: when a
mature core can't express your

100
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:36,010
problem, don't fork the core.

101
00:06:36,571 --> 00:06:42,244
Put a decomposition or reassembly layer
at the edge and keep the core boring.

102
00:06:43,244 --> 00:06:48,520
Decomposition gets the pod into the
auction, but the rules that make a pod

103
00:06:48,586 --> 00:06:51,853
correct live one level up at reassembly.

104
00:06:52,323 --> 00:06:56,638
Because the spin impressions and
a per-impression auction can't

105
00:06:56,664 --> 00:07:03,423
see across its own boundary, deal
opportunity assignment fills the

106
00:07:03,423 --> 00:07:06,112
pod honoring deal tiers first.

107
00:07:06,590 --> 00:07:13,335
Committed deals get what their tier
promises, and the open auction competes

108
00:07:13,355 --> 00:07:15,358
for the positions that are left.

109
00:07:15,885 --> 00:07:19,667
A pod is one portfolio decision,
not five independent ones.

110
00:07:21,452 --> 00:07:25,850
Two car ads in one break is a failure
the viewer notices, even if every

111
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:27,405
individual auction was correct.

112
00:07:27,908 --> 00:07:31,140
So separation is enforced
across the assembled pod.

113
00:07:31,421 --> 00:07:34,452
The pod is a unit of
correctness, not the slot.

114
00:07:35,508 --> 00:07:36,438
Frequency control.

115
00:07:36,484 --> 00:07:40,970
The same ad twice in one break or
over and over across an evening

116
00:07:41,365 --> 00:07:42,829
is the same class of failure.

117
00:07:43,123 --> 00:07:47,766
Frequency state can't live inside
a stateless per-impression core.

118
00:07:48,452 --> 00:07:55,168
So cross-pod rule like it ride shared
state at the edge layer, checked and

119
00:07:55,178 --> 00:07:57,433
updated as each pod is assembled.

120
00:07:58,231 --> 00:08:04,984
The pattern to steal: any rule that
spans requests belongs at the layer

121
00:08:05,099 --> 00:08:07,415
that can see the whole unit of work.

122
00:08:07,904 --> 00:08:13,205
Push it down into the core, and core stops
being boring and stops being reusable

123
00:08:14,205 --> 00:08:16,850
Here is a full architecture in four lanes.

124
00:08:17,354 --> 00:08:21,931
Top lane, the live auction,
latency critical, the publisher

125
00:08:21,931 --> 00:08:24,104
or app offers one impression.

126
00:08:24,542 --> 00:08:26,914
The exchange fans out the request.

127
00:08:27,223 --> 00:08:32,244
The geo edge and load balancer route
to a healthy region, and the stateless

128
00:08:32,272 --> 00:08:35,031
bidder fleet scores and returns a bid.

129
00:08:35,662 --> 00:08:41,190
Inside that lane sits the bounded
dependencies, the feature cache

130
00:08:41,203 --> 00:08:45,434
for user, and context budget
data for campaign availability.

131
00:08:46,114 --> 00:08:51,637
The serving model with its pre-computed
value, every parallel call stops

132
00:08:51,694 --> 00:08:56,910
at its deadline, missing data falls
back, and the lane ends in a bid or

133
00:08:56,915 --> 00:08:59,535
a no bid returned before timeout.

134
00:09:00,328 --> 00:09:05,640
Below that, the event stream
asynchronously like every auction outcome,

135
00:09:06,095 --> 00:09:11,475
win-loss impression goes through a
pub/sub, which decouples downstream work

136
00:09:11,499 --> 00:09:14,609
into logging, billing, and training data.

137
00:09:15,001 --> 00:09:17,484
The live path never waits for any of it.

138
00:09:18,295 --> 00:09:24,189
And the bottom lane of f-- offline
learning compute heavy, the outcome

139
00:09:24,191 --> 00:09:26,444
stores collects labeled results.

140
00:09:26,829 --> 00:09:31,489
The training pipeline retrains outside
the request path and published models

141
00:09:31,498 --> 00:09:33,560
and features refresh the serving layer.

142
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:38,537
The learning loop improves the live
path without ever standing in it.

143
00:09:39,537 --> 00:09:43,539
How a single request moves through
that live lane in four steps.

144
00:09:43,622 --> 00:09:49,212
Once receive the request, parse the
impression and start one shared deadline.

145
00:09:50,074 --> 00:09:55,436
Second, fan out in parallel to multiple
services like user context, campaign

146
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:57,641
budget, and model inputs fetched together.

147
00:09:58,726 --> 00:10:02,561
Three, what-- join what is
returned from the previous step.

148
00:10:02,946 --> 00:10:06,821
Stop waiting for any-- when each
sub-call reaches its timeout budget.

149
00:10:07,572 --> 00:10:09,470
The last one, decide and respond.

150
00:10:09,479 --> 00:10:14,820
Use the available signals to return a
bid or no bid before the exchange closes.

151
00:10:15,828 --> 00:10:19,604
And the failure table on
the right is a real design.

152
00:10:20,411 --> 00:10:23,491
Cache miss, read the source,
then repopulate the cache.

153
00:10:24,204 --> 00:10:27,935
Like a slow lookup, use the
same default and continue.

154
00:10:28,595 --> 00:10:32,623
Repeated failures, open the
circuit and stop adding load.

155
00:10:33,748 --> 00:10:38,167
The deadline is ex-exhausted, return
no bid rather than a late response.

156
00:10:38,948 --> 00:10:41,689
Timeouts are a design
input, not an error case.

157
00:10:41,902 --> 00:10:44,863
A missing signal is cheaper
than a missed auction.

158
00:10:45,443 --> 00:10:49,953
Degradation is deliberate, rehearsed,
and observable, never accidental.

159
00:10:50,953 --> 00:10:54,520
You keep healthy by watching
the tail, not the average.

160
00:10:54,743 --> 00:10:59,151
The average hides exactly the requests
most likely to miss the auction.

161
00:10:59,181 --> 00:11:07,602
P95, P99, P999 are the product metrics,
but measurement itself spends the budget.

162
00:11:07,646 --> 00:11:12,460
So observability is sampled,
aggregated at the edge, and shipped

163
00:11:12,497 --> 00:11:14,408
asynchronously on the event lane.

164
00:11:14,569 --> 00:11:15,977
The live path emits.

165
00:11:16,361 --> 00:11:18,549
It never waits to report.

166
00:11:19,243 --> 00:11:20,527
Then the loop closes.

167
00:11:20,654 --> 00:11:23,161
Every auction outcome is published.

168
00:11:23,644 --> 00:11:28,507
Metrics and traces correlate,
model, retrain offline, and

169
00:11:28,513 --> 00:11:31,647
serving data refreshes safely.

170
00:11:31,861 --> 00:11:35,889
Every release signals become
the next architecture decision

171
00:11:36,889 --> 00:11:40,488
Four lessons, and none of them
are a tech, a tech specific.

172
00:11:40,747 --> 00:11:47,056
One, given every dependency a deadline
and a default, unbounded, unbounded

173
00:11:47,076 --> 00:11:50,161
waits are how strict budget systems die.

174
00:11:51,107 --> 00:11:55,275
Two, separate the deadline
from the learning loop.

175
00:11:55,522 --> 00:11:59,967
If a request path waits on training
analytics or reporting, the

176
00:11:59,994 --> 00:12:02,798
architecture, not the code, is the bug.

177
00:12:04,424 --> 00:12:10,238
Three, design degradation as a feature
with a owner, a metric, and a rehearsal,

178
00:12:10,238 --> 00:12:12,640
not as an apology after an incident.

179
00:12:14,007 --> 00:12:18,189
The last one, watch the tail and
budget your observability like

180
00:12:18,189 --> 00:12:20,153
your budget to your compute.

181
00:12:20,973 --> 00:12:25,535
Any platform with a hard latency wall,
payments, search recommendations,

182
00:12:26,232 --> 00:12:30,706
converges on the same moves because
the deadline doesn't negotiate.

183
00:12:31,706 --> 00:12:32,607
That's a story.

184
00:12:32,841 --> 00:12:37,385
One deadline, one naive design,
and an architecture built

185
00:12:37,434 --> 00:12:38,782
one constraint at a time.

186
00:12:39,358 --> 00:12:40,972
Thank you for the time.

187
00:12:41,119 --> 00:12:42,164
I'm Sujan Challa.

188
00:12:42,722 --> 00:12:46,754
If you're fighting a latency
wall of your own, I would

189
00:12:46,754 --> 00:12:49,027
genuinely like to compare notes.

190
00:12:49,064 --> 00:12:54,456
Find me in the, conference chat or
connect with me, on the socials.

191
00:12:55,860 --> 00:13:01,399
May your deadlines be a ho-- as honest
as this one, and may you never pay

192
00:13:01,408 --> 00:13:03,312
retail for the millisecond again.

193
00:13:04,052 --> 00:13:04,600
Thank you.
